1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,560
Packages gave our types a home. That does
not yet solve a quieter mess that shows up
the moment a domain has a fixed
vocabulary. Suppose we are building an
order system. An order can be new, paid,
or shipped.

2
00:00:13,679 --> 00:00:26,960
The fastest way to encode that is a string
field: "NEW", "PAID", "SHIPPED". It
compiles. It demos well. Then someone
writes "Payed", or "paid", or "SHIPED".
The compiler shrugs.

3
00:00:27,079 --> 00:00:33,799
The bug arrives in production as a status
that matches nothing — a branch never
taken, a warehouse never notified.

4
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:48,840
So a natural question appears: if the set
of legal values is known and closed, why
are we letting arbitrary text pretend to
be a state? That question is why enums
exist. An enum is a type-safe set of named
constants.

5
00:00:48,960 --> 00:01:02,679
Instead of magic strings or magic ints,
you get a vocabulary the compiler
understands. Look at this java for a
moment. Status is now a real type. A
method that takes Status cannot
accidentally receive "SHIPED".

6
00:01:02,799 --> 00:01:09,319
The allowed values live in one place.
Interviewers sometimes ask why enums beat
public static final int.

7
00:01:09,439 --> 00:01:20,319
The short answer is type safety,
namespacing, and room for attached
behavior — an int constant named PAID is
still just another int that can be
confused with a quantity or an HTTP code.

8
00:01:20,439 --> 00:01:30,239
But enums in Java are not only labels.
Once you have a named state, behavior
often wants to hang off that state. Is
this status terminal? Can we refund from
here?

9
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:40,460
That pressure turns the enum into a small
class with fields and methods. Look at
this java for a moment. NEW, PAID, and
SHIPPED are the only instances of Status.

10
00:01:40,579 --> 00:01:49,620
terminal() answers a domain question
without a scattered if chain of strings.
Call sites write order.status().terminal()
and stay readable.

11
00:01:49,739 --> 00:01:59,619
You can also give constants fields — a
display label, a sort rank — as long as
you treat that data as part of the fixed
vocabulary, not as a mutable global.

12
00:01:59,739 --> 00:02:13,979
Enums also shine with switch, because the
set of cases is finite. Modern Java can
warn or error when a switch is not
exhaustive. Look at this java for a
moment. Each case is a real constant, not
a quoted guess.

13
00:02:14,099 --> 00:02:26,900
If someone later adds CANCELLED to the
enum, the incomplete switch becomes a
compile-time problem instead of a silent
default path. Model states explicitly,
then let the compiler herd every place
that must react.

14
00:02:27,019 --> 00:02:42,819
What if we skip enums and keep stringly-
typed status codes? Look at this java for
a moment. It looks small. Then "Paid"
sneaks in from a form, or a partner API
sends "paid". Equality fails. The order
never ships.

15
00:02:42,939 --> 00:02:57,459
Mutable enum fields cause a different
surprise: if you stash changing data on an
enum constant, every caller shares that
mutation. Prefer enums as immutable
vocabulary with behavior, not as disguised
global variables.

16
00:02:57,579 --> 00:03:09,179
There is one more practical detail once
enums become common: collections
specialized for them. EnumSet and EnumMap
are fast, compact structures tuned for
enum keys and values.

17
00:03:09,299 --> 00:03:21,139
If you need a set of flags that are
themselves enum constants, reach for
EnumSet before a general HashSet. Let's
put the enum inside a small order story so
the vocabulary has somewhere to live.

18
00:03:21,259 --> 00:03:30,179
Look at this java for a moment. The field
is a Status, not a String. markPaid
refuses nonsense transitions instead of
comparing quoted text.

19
00:03:30,299 --> 00:03:42,459
When you later add CANCELLED, every place
that switches on status() becomes a
checklist the compiler can help enforce.
We started with status strings that
compiled and lied. Enums answered with
type-safe constants.

20
00:03:42,579 --> 00:03:48,259
Fields and methods let vocabulary carry
behavior. Switch made exhaustive handling
natural.

21
00:03:48,379 --> 00:04:02,499
Once constants can be objects, another
pressure appears: collections and APIs
often want objects, while hot paths still
want primitives. How does an int sit
inside a List? That bridge is wrappers and
autoboxing.
